course: chinese-street
lesson: 11
title: Dinner at the friend's house
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: From the kitchen Ming's mum calls «chī fàn le!» (dinner's ready!), literally «eating rice now». You shout «lái le!» (coming!) and go at once — waiting to be called twice is rude.
explain: On the table there is mǐfàn (rice) in a bowl each, which you pick up and hold near your mouth — good manners here, not messy like at school dinners. Then dishes in the middle for everyone: the famous xīhóngshì chǎo jīdàn (tomato and egg), dòufu, maybe Granny's sticky hóngshāoròu.
explain: Some days there are jiǎozi (dumplings), miàntiáo (noodles) to slurp, or plain mántou buns; breakfast may be zhōu, a salty rice porridge, nothing like British porridge.
explain: And the kuàizi (chopsticks) have their own rules: never stand them upright in the rice, never point them at people, never drum on the bowl.
fact: chī fàn le («CHR FAHN luh») means dinner's ready; fàn means rice and also meal, and the answer is lái le! (coming!).
fact: mǐfàn («MEE-fahn») is cooked rice, a bowl each at nearly every meal; lifting the bowl near your mouth is good manners.
fact: jiǎozi («JYOW-dz», dipping) are boiled dumplings with meat or vegetable filling, made by families together, especially at New Year.
fact: mántou («MAHN?-toe») is a plain steamed bun with no filling; with a filling it is a bāozi.
fact: miàntiáo («MYEN-tyow?») are noodles; slurping is fine, and on birthdays long noodles are not cut, because they mean long life.
fact: chǎofàn («CHAOW-fahn») is fried rice, at home often made from yesterday's rice and whatever is in the fridge.
fact: zhōu («joe», high) is rice porridge (congee), eaten for breakfast or when ill; it is salty, with pickles or egg on top.
fact: dòufu («DOE-foo») is tofu, soft or firm; chòu dòufu (stinky tofu) really does smell, and friends may dare you to try it.
fact: xīhóngshì chǎo jīdàn («SHEE-hong?-shr CHAOW JEE-dahn») is tomato and egg stir-fry, the most famous home dish, which every family cooks a little differently; some families call tomato fānqié.
fact: hóngshāoròu («HONG?-shaow-ROE») is red-braised pork belly, sweet and glossy; families who don't eat pork, such as some Muslim Chinese families or vegetarians, won't cook it, so don't ask for it.
fact: kuàizi («KWIGH-dz») are chopsticks; upright in rice they look like incense at a funeral, and pointing them or tapping the bowl is bad manners.
exercise: What does «chī fàn le!» mean? | Dinner's ready! | I'm full! | Wash your hands! | Go to bed!
exercise: What should you do when you hear chī fàn le? | Shout lái le! and go at once | Wait politely until you're called twice | Finish your game first, then go | Ask what's for dinner
exercise: What is mǐfàn? | Cooked rice | Noodles | A sweet bun | Soup
exercise: Picking up your rice bowl near your mouth at Ming's table is | Good manners | Messy and rude | Only for babies | Only for grandparents
exercise: What are jiǎozi? | Boiled dumplings with a filling | Plain steamed buns with nothing inside | Fried noodles | Thin rice porridge with pickles
exercise: What is mántou? | A plain steamed bun with no filling | A soft bun filled with sweet pork and onion | A dumpling soup | A sticky rice cake for New Year
exercise: Why don't people cut long noodles on birthdays? | Long noodles mean long life | Cutting noodles is bad for the knives | Noodles taste worse cut short | It makes the cook angry and sad
exercise: chǎofàn is | Fried rice | Fried noodles | Plain rice | Rice porridge
exercise: How is zhōu different from British porridge? | It is salty, with pickles or egg | It is made of oats and milk, like ours | It is always sweet, with jam on top | It is eaten cold
exercise: dòufu is | Tofu | Pork | Egg | Bread
exercise: What is xīhóngshì chǎo jīdàn? | Tomato and egg stir-fry | Fried rice with ham | Egg drop soup | Sweet tomato jam
exercise: Some families call the tomato | fānqié | mántou | dòufu | kělè
exercise: What is hóngshāoròu? | Red-braised pork belly | Red beans in soup | Roast duck | Spicy tofu
exercise: Why won't some families cook hóngshāoròu? | They don't eat pork | It is too spicy | It is only for restaurants | It is bad luck
exercise: What are kuàizi? | Chopsticks | Bowls | Spoons | Napkins
exercise: Why should you not stand chopsticks upright in your rice? | It looks like incense at a funeral | They might fall over and splash the rice | It means you are full and want to go | It blocks the view
exercise: When might you get zhōu? | Breakfast, or when you're ill | Only at weddings and big parties | As a sweet pudding after dinner | Only at New Year
test: «ming's mum shouted chī fàn le but i was mid game so i waited» What should you have done? | Gone at once; waiting to be called twice is rude | Waited, it's polite to finish whatever you're doing | Asked for dinner in your room | Shouted bú yào, so she knows you're busy
test: You stick your chopsticks straight up in the rice to free your hands. Why does Granny frown? | It looks like funeral incense | It means you hate her rice | It's a rude word in China, said with hands | It means «more please»
test: «zhōu is porridge so where's the sugar» What's right? | It's salty congee, with pickles or egg | It always comes with jam, just ask | It's a sweet pudding with red beans | It's a kind of noodle
test: At Ming's birthday you cut his noodles to make them easier. What might Granny say? | Long noodles mean long life, don't cut them | Well done, that's very polite and thoughtful of you | Cut them smaller, he's only little | Noodles are for adults
test: «ask for hóngshāoròu, it's the best» at a friend's who doesn't eat pork. What's right? | Don't ask; they won't cook it, and that's fine | Ask loudly, they'll make it specially for a guest | Bring some yourself in a box | Tell them it's rude not to cook it
test: You drum on your bowl with your kuàizi waiting for food. What do the grown-ups think? | It's bad manners | It's a fun game | It means you're full | It's how you say thank you
test: «dare ya to eat chòu dòufu» What is it? | Stinky tofu, which really smells | Rotten eggs kept for a hundred years | Spicy noodles | Burnt rice from the bottom of the pot
test: In a cartoon someone bites into a mántou and finds pork inside. What was it really? | A bāozi; mántou has no filling | A jiǎozi | A zhōu | A kuàizi
test: What would happen if you shouted «lái le!» when Ming's mum calls dinner? | All good: it means «coming!» | It means «go away» | It means «I'm not hungry» | It means «what's for dinner?»
test: «tomato and egg again, every family makes it the same» What is true? | Every family makes it a bit differently | Everyone follows one official recipe from school | Only restaurants make it | It is a sweet dessert
test: You hold your rice bowl up close at Ming's. Ming's dad smiles. Why? | That's good manners at a Chinese table | He thinks you're messy | He wants you to put it down | He thinks you're cold
exam: Identify the customary meaning of the announcement chī fàn le. | That the meal is ready and all are summoned to the table | That the speaker has finished eating and may now leave the table | That rice is to be purchased from the market | That the guest is invited to depart
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which chopsticks should not be placed upright in a bowl of rice. | The arrangement resembles incense offered at funerals | It damages the chopsticks irreparably | It signifies that the meal is unsatisfactory to the guest | It obstructs other diners
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning zhōu. | It is a savoury rice porridge eaten at breakfast or in illness | It is a sweet oat porridge served with milk and sugar | It is a fried noodle dish | It is a festive dumpling
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the distinction between mántou and bāozi? | The former is a plain steamed bun; the latter has a filling | The former contains a filling, whereas the latter is plain | Both denote boiled dumplings | Both denote fried rice
